WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.189 --> 00:00:17.060
Tim Ferriss: All right, John, nice to see
you, and welcome to the show.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.100
John Romero: Well, thanks for having me.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.100
I'm excited.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.630
Tim Ferriss: I thought I would give a few
caveats for people listening.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:28.470
Number one, I'm going to operate on the assumption
that most people have very little context

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.780
on your life story, so I will be asking a
lot of very basic questions.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.559
And I extend my apologies to you and the hardcore
gamers out there, but I think that'll be helpful

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:40.559
for folks.

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:47.750
And some very, very long-term listeners may
recognize your name because it came up in

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:53.390
conversation with Alexis Ohanian, who is a
friend of mine, co-founder of Reddit, who

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:57.500
credits your story with helping inspire the
creation of that company.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:03.840
And of course your story and the products
of your imagination and hard work have also

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.150
inspired many, many others.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:13.880
The creation of Oculus VR and the cultural
touchstones and the influence of pop culture

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.950
over decades is really remarkable.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:21.200
So in the process of doing my homework and
due diligence for this conversation, the expansive

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.369
nature of the impact is pretty mind-boggling.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:32.630
But I want to start in the beginning, and
by beginning I mean childhood, because I'd

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.729
like to provide some connective tissue that
then leads into later questions.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:41.850
Could you please just describe where you grew
up and what your childhood looked like?

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:47.970
John Romero: I grew up in Tucson, Arizona.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:55.010
I was born in '67, so I did a lot of my early
years in the late '60s and the early '70s.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:02:07.090
I grew up right next to the Yaqui Reservation,
and my families, on my dad's side, has Aztec

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:16.050
and Yaqui heritage, but we call ourselves
Mexicans and Mexican food was the food that

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.360
we all ate.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:23.800
We lived in a pretty poor place.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:32.620
I only lived in that area until about fourth
grade, but it was pretty culturally significant

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:40.019
to me because I really learned a lot of my
Mexican side at that time.

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:41.019
And then we moved.

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:50.050
My dad went to the grocery store and never
came back, and so my mom got a divorce and

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:56.650
then met a guy that was a military guy and
got married.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.319
And then we moved to Northern California,
which was very, very different than the desert

00:03:01.319 --> 00:03:02.409
in Tucson, Arizona.

00:03:02.409 --> 00:03:11.239
Tim Ferriss: I would love to hear you expand
a little bit on your time in Tucson and your

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:15.860
experience with your biological father because
I think it paints perhaps a picture.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:16.860
I was reading a piece in The Washington Post
that certainly made it seem very challenging,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:17.860
that chapter of your life.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.610
John Romero: I don't know how many generations
ago that is, but in human generations it was

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.630
a long time ago and people definitely acted
differently back then.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:35.099
And I guess in the family and in just socially,
the circles that my dad ran around in, drinking

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:38.189
alcohol was like drinking water.

00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:41.989
I never saw him actually drink water, it was
always beer.

00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:46.409
And it wasn't just him, it was basically just
about everybody.

00:03:46.409 --> 00:03:50.439
And people that drink usually will surround
themselves with other people that drink because

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:55.239
they like doing similar things together.

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:57.909
So everybody that I saw always drank.

00:03:57.909 --> 00:04:02.489
And that always led to all kinds of crazy
stuff happening, because when you've been

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:05.379
drinking, you're going to be doing all kinds
of random stuff.

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:07.260
INTERLUDE
Tim Ferriss [to listeners]: At this point,

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:15.569
I think it might be helpful to provide a little
bit of context related to the hardships that

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:18.440
John endured and overcame in his childhood.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:24.650
Because I think it informs who he is and speaks
to a toughness and resilience that is worth

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:25.650
underscoring.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:31.620
So what I’m going to read is an excerpt
from a very good piece in The Washington Post

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:37.919
titled “Why revered developers John and
Brenda Romero started a game studio in Ireland.”

00:04:37.919 --> 00:04:40.280
And here is that excerpt.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.060
“John grew up in Tucson, Arizona.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.169
His biological father was an alcoholic.

00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:48.340
‘At that point in time, John would've had
burn marks on his arms, he would've seen his

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:50.980
mother getting beat up,” Brenda said.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.150
[Brenda is John’s wife.]

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.150
Money and food were tight.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.830
John’s father robbed stores at gunpoint
on multiple occasions to get diapers.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:01.050
One day, when John and his younger brother
Ralph made a mess in the kitchen, his mother,

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.240
stressed, told John’s father to ‘get rid
of them.’

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.880
It wasn’t meant to be literal.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.650
‘He just put us in the truck and drove us
out in the middle of the desert and told us

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.240
to get out and then took off,’ John said.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.240
He was six.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:14.289
Ralph was four.

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:17.540
John recognized where they were and decided
to follow the truck, spending a half-hour

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:20.650
in the desert until his mother made his father
bring them home.”

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:25.830
So this is one of quite a number of examples,
but I think it is worth including here simply

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:30.590
because it is part of the context that makes
John who he is.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:31.870
And with that, back to the interview.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:33.650
END INTERLUDE
Tim Ferriss [in conversation]: It seems like

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:42.150
when your mom met this military man, that
it was a real significant chapter shift and

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:46.139
shift of environment, shift of experience.

00:05:46.139 --> 00:05:52.790
And I'm not sure how far ahead we'd be skipping,
but could you explain what Aggressor Squadron

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:56.710
was and how you became involved with that?

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:03.660
John Romero: The Aggressor Squadron was one
of the divisions on the military base that

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:07.860
he was stationed at in 1983.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:14.669
That was a place where the pilots — this
is the Cold War, by the way.

00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:20.360
The pilots are learning Russian flight maneuvers,
combat maneuvers, and the Aggressor Squadron

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:27.349
is where they all learned all of the flight
training, I guess, that they needed in case

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:29.550
they got in contact with Russians.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:34.260
And the point was not to ever get in contact,
because this space was primarily a reconnaissance

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:41.039
space where they were flying U-2s and TR-1s
to do reconnaissance over Russia, to try and

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:42.569
find out what they're doing.

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:45.050
But in case something happened, they would
know what would to do.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:47.770
So they flew a lot of other planes out of
there.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:48.770
I think F-5s, a lot of F-5s.

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.069
And anyway, that was the place where he did
that.

00:06:51.069 --> 00:06:56.490
When I started school halfway through — at
the beginning of 1983 but was halfway through

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:06.979
the school year of my sophomore year in school,
I could actually choose to take computer programming,

00:07:06.979 --> 00:07:09.759
which is pretty great in the '80s.

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:15.460
Because it was halfway through the year, the
teacher had to approve that it was okay for

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.850
me to join because I was halfway through learning
BASIC.

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:22.729
But I already knew BASIC, I was finished with
BASIC and I was learning assembly language.

00:07:22.729 --> 00:07:26.940
And when I talked to the teacher and showed
her that I knew assembly and that's why I'm

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:32.440
coding and I know everything about this machine
and all that, so she was really impressed

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.780
the first day that I got into school.

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:41.569
And the second day when I came to school,
she told everyone in the class what to work

00:07:41.569 --> 00:07:47.259
on and then she told me to follow her, and
we went to her car and she drove me to the

00:07:47.259 --> 00:07:54.509
Aggressor Squadron on base and introduced
me to Captain Spencer.

00:07:54.509 --> 00:07:56.490
And he was Top Gun dude.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:08:01.009
He was a total rocket jockey guy.

00:08:01.009 --> 00:08:06.630
He ejected out over the Mediterranean, this
guy's done so much stuff.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:13.840
Anyway, he went to a phone, and he said a
bunch of different code words into the phone.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:24.490
And then this giant bank vault-looking door
opened, big round one, it opened and we went

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:27.180
inside of this vault vault, a bank vault-looking
thing.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:30.699
It was in the middle of the Aggressor Squadron
where they keep classified stuff.

00:08:30.699 --> 00:08:35.339
And they had already put away the stuff because
they knew that he was bringing someone in

00:08:35.339 --> 00:08:36.339
that didn't have clearance.

00:08:36.339 --> 00:08:43.550
And he took me to the back and I saw this
mini computer, it was a Cromemco, and he said,

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:45.440
"Here's our mini computer.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.840
I'm just wondering if you knew how to program
it?"

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:55.959
And so I got on it and I noticed that it was
using the CPM operating system and that you

00:08:55.959 --> 00:08:57.240
could program it in BASIC.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.350
So I said, "Yeah, yeah, I know CPM and I know
BASIC."

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:04.260
He is like, "You've got yourself a job."

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:10.579
So basically I got a job the second day I
got to school just because of connections.

00:09:10.579 --> 00:09:16.649
My teacher saw that I knew how to code and
then she ran into this guy later that day

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:20.160
and told him about me and he is like, "Bring
him on over."

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.340
So it was just really, really random.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:28.740
Tim Ferriss: How did you get your very first
taste of computers?

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.790
When did you get bitten by the bug?

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:37.399
Given the humble beginnings where one would
assume, especially at that time, that it would

00:09:37.399 --> 00:09:41.380
be very hard perhaps to come across or get
access to computers.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:46.940
John Romero: Getting access to computers in
the '70s was basically not going to ever happen

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:48.899
in someone's house.

00:09:48.899 --> 00:09:50.019
Computers weren't even in the home yet.

00:09:50.019 --> 00:09:56.709
The personal computer revolution was just
barely beginning, so only thousands of people

00:09:56.709 --> 00:10:02.780
in the United States may have had a computer
and they most likely built it themselves.

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:08.600
So computers were very early, they weren't
really in houses yet.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:12.990
But in 1979, my friend — I was addicted
to arcades.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:17.800
I played arcade games all the time, or pinball
or anything, because it was so much fun and

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:18.880
so different.

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:25.829
But it cost money and it's hard for a kid
who's 10 or 11 to actually have the money

00:10:25.829 --> 00:10:28.190
to spend on arcades.

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:33.870
But my friend came to my house and said, "I
just found a way for us to play games that

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:34.870
doesn't cost any money."

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:36.380
And I'm like, "Oh, my God."

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:42.500
I jumped on my bike and I followed him, and
about a half an hour later we pedal up to

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:46.300
the Sierra College computer lab.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:51.080
It's summertime in 1979, so I'm 11 years old.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:58.020
And I just follow him and we go into this
computer lab, it's really nice and cold.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:05.399
And basically he shows me this computer and
the computer, it's a mainframe in a room.

00:11:05.399 --> 00:11:09.430
There's a room that's just got a giant computer
in it and there's these terminals, these black

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:14.290
and white TV screens with keyboards connected
to them.

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:17.380
And I mean, there's like one unit.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:23.600
And so he starts showing me these really early
text games, and these text games are Hunt

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:29.959
the Wumpus and Star Trek and Poison Cookie
and these just really early, early games.

00:11:29.959 --> 00:11:35.240
And I was just really impressed because I
didn't need to pump in quarters and I could

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:40.100
take my time with every move, I could just
think about it and then move.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.560
That was very, very different.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:47.089
So I played for a little while and I noticed
that there were only probably two students

00:11:47.089 --> 00:11:51.540
in the room, and I said, "Well, what are they
doing on the computer, because it doesn't

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:52.750
look like they're playing?"

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.570
He goes, "Oh, no, they're programming."

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.380
And I'm like, "What is that?"

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:58.940
He said, "Well, that's actually what you do
to make these games.

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:04.330
You have to learn how to tell the computer
how to put stuff on the screen and what to

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:05.880
do, how to think."

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.839
And I'm like, "I want to do that."

00:12:07.839 --> 00:12:13.160
So I just went over and asked one of the students,
"What does that mean?"

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:16.490
And it's like print statement and some other
stuff.

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:19.889
And he tells me what it is and I started asking
him some more questions.

00:12:19.889 --> 00:12:23.149
"What does 'if' do, what does that mean?"

00:12:23.149 --> 00:12:24.820
And so he is explaining it.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:30.490
And eventually, because I'm asking too many
questions, he just gets me the book for BASIC

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:37.210
that's there and I just take it over to the
computer that I was on and I basically started

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:38.769
learning how to code.

00:12:38.769 --> 00:12:44.940
And with nobody kicking me off the computer,
I'm sitting there with a book and I'm starting

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:51.101
to teach myself how to program a really basic
adventure, which goes from room to room, just

00:12:51.101 --> 00:12:52.190
with a text description.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:55.449
So basically, on a high level, it's kind of
crazy.

00:12:55.449 --> 00:13:00.790
11-year-old goes to computer lab at the college
and teaches himself how to code.

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:03.700
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's incredible.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.260
John Romero: And so yeah, that's what I did.

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:08.329
And I didn't have a computer for three years
after that.

00:13:08.329 --> 00:13:14.430
In the meantime I would go to any computer
store in my town or the next town, and if

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:19.080
they had a computer there, like Radio Shack
has a TRS-80 or something, I would sit down

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:22.650
and I would find one of the books that has
programming in it and I would just enter the

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:28.470
code into the computer and play around with
it and figure out if it doesn't work usually,

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:31.880
why it doesn't work, and try to fix it and
then change it.

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:34.839
And that's really slow learning.

00:13:34.839 --> 00:13:38.870
But that was how I learned how to code in
my first introduction to computers was at

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:42.060
the college because I never saw them anywhere
else.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:43.519
Tim Ferriss: Pedaling over.

00:13:43.519 --> 00:13:52.140
And was the main interest, at that point,
I would assume the creation of games?

00:13:52.140 --> 00:14:00.040
And I guess the follow on to that is, was
there something about programming that tickled

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:02.269
your brain in a particular way?

00:14:02.269 --> 00:14:08.130
Was there something else intrinsic to programming
that appealed to you beyond the output, which

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:09.380
would be the games?

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:15.100
John Romero: Gaming was the only reason why
I wanted to start learning how to code because

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:19.650
I — thinking about arcade games, it was
like, "I have no idea how I would even make

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:20.650
that."

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:25.880
But looking at the games that I had played
on the mainframe was like, "I could probably

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:29.360
do that," because that's just really simple
stuff on the screen.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:33.921
So when I could get things up on the screen
quickly and they kind of looked similar to

00:14:33.921 --> 00:14:36.890
what I was playing, I was like, "I am totally
on the right track.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.130
This is going to work."

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:42.860
So the interest was only to make games.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:48.290
But one of the really interesting things about
programming, and especially back then, was

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:53.490
that the programming experience was extremely
immediate.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:57.891
On computers back then you could turn the
computer on and it was ready to code, right

00:14:57.891 --> 00:14:58.891
then.

00:14:58.891 --> 00:15:02.960
There was no programs to run to code a computer,
it was ready.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:10.380
So the iteration speed of learning was as
fast as it gets.

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:13.529
It's like, I could type something in and run
it.

00:15:13.529 --> 00:15:18.040
And it either was going to bomb and tell me
there's something wrong or it's going to work

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:19.980
and I'm like, "I just learned something new."

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:25.510
So that iterative, that fast feedback, iterative
nature of programming, was really, really

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:30.399
conducive to keep my interest in learning
how to code and get better at it.

00:15:30.399 --> 00:15:37.160
And yeah, it was just building on stuff very,
very fast.

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:40.779
Learning all the keywords, figuring out how
to put them all together to do certain things.

00:15:40.779 --> 00:15:46.940
And not just solving problems, but I'm starting
to architect what I want to do.

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:53.029
And so there's these phases of learning where
you start to understand that anything that

00:15:53.029 --> 00:15:55.110
you can think of, you can make it happen.

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:59.731
If you only you have the right words and the
right description to, "How do I put a thing

00:15:59.731 --> 00:16:00.731
on the screen?"

00:16:00.731 --> 00:16:01.731
Well, that's with print.

00:16:01.731 --> 00:16:03.370
"How do I put it over there on the screen?"

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:07.089
And then it's like, there's some commands
for saying exactly how to get to that point

00:16:07.089 --> 00:16:08.199
on the screen.

00:16:08.199 --> 00:16:13.329
When you start breaking down all of these
little problems and you know how to solve

00:16:13.329 --> 00:16:17.240
one of these little problems, you start to
have a lot of confidence that you can do what

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:18.970
you're hoping to do.

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:21.170
And that just makes you want to keep on going.

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:25.950
So solving problems really, if you're a person
who likes to solve problems, programming is

00:16:25.950 --> 00:16:32.339
absolutely for you because your job and your
whole life is solving problems when you are

00:16:32.339 --> 00:16:33.430
a beginning programmer.

00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:38.699
You're climbing these walls constantly to
get over them and learn something and go do

00:16:38.699 --> 00:16:40.959
the new thing.

00:16:40.959 --> 00:16:46.029
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to leap forward a bit
and then we're going to go back and fill in

00:16:46.029 --> 00:16:47.750
some of the gaps.

00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:53.480
And I'm getting very much in the deep end
of my ignorance pool, so I'm going to need

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.990
you to lead me through what I'm about to read.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:17:07.079
But I was talking to my brother last night
who codes, is involved with technology, and

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:13.589
he pointed me ultimately to a rabbit hole
that led me to a paragraph, this is going

00:17:13.589 --> 00:17:19.110
somewhere, on gamerant.com, and I was hoping
you could explain what this means.

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:23.360
So, "John Romero ended up making the biggest
advancement in gaming since the invention

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:24.360
of the video game itself.

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:29.799
He was the first person to make a navigable
maze appear on a screen, giving gaming a third

00:17:29.799 --> 00:17:32.299
dimension of movement."

00:17:32.299 --> 00:17:38.360
We were just talking about problem solving,
and this may require some explanation on your

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:44.360
part, but could you walk us through the significance
of that and how it came to be?

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:50.640
John Romero: Computer games have been around
since the '70s.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:55.640
And 3D games have also been around since the
'70s, so there were some really primitive

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:56.640
games.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:00.590
But first-person, if we're talking about first-person,
which is where people really start to get

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:10.910
into 3D, there was really, really early games
in the — say, in 1974, is where 3D games

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:16.740
really start on mainframe computers, where
it's rendering a hallway scene and maybe a

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:19.179
character on the screen in front of you.

00:18:19.179 --> 00:18:22.600
And when you move, you don't move smoothly,
you move by a chunk of the maze.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:27.110
So it's a really primitive movement, but it's
like, it's rendered in 3D.

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:33.330
And not until we get to, say, Battlezone in
1980 does it become very smooth.

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:39.390
And Battlezone is line-drawn pyramids and
stuff in the back in the scene and, say, another

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:40.390
tank.

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:43.880
You're in a tank and there's another tank,
and they're smoothly moving and coming towards

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:49.840
you and you're having to use tank controls
to aim and shoot at it, and everything's coming

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:50.840
for you.

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:54.650
But it was a smooth-moving 3D, but it was
line-drawn.

00:18:54.650 --> 00:19:00.450
So the advancements in 3D eventually get to
the point where you have solid filled walls

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:06.080
so it looks like a color, walls look a certain
color, so when you turn it's not seeing through

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:08.160
an invisible wall.

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:13.260
And then they start getting textures in the
mid '80s.

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:17.380
So if you play, say, Might &amp; Magic, you'll
see it.

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:22.820
I think some Wizardry games also had it.

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:29.980
And even Ultima, when you go into the dungeons,
you'll see a rendered 3D view that has texture

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:34.260
on it, like someone drew a wall, like an actual
bricks or whatever.

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:36.720
But it doesn't move smoothly, it's moving
by chunks.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:41.230
Still, on computers, it was a lot slower to
get to that point of smooth movement.

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:51.630
So when we started doing 3D in 1991, we did
do solid color walls, but we could move through

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:59.840
that maze perfectly smooth and turn any direction
we wanted to and see that maze in fluid, full,

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:04.900
high-speed 3D, which nobody had seen at that
point.

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:11.120
Our focus was on trying to make that happen,
the speed was the focus for us, getting 3D

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:17.200
moving quickly became — at least at that
point with that one early game, that was really

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:18.200
important for us.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:24.230
It wasn't to do something really amazing with
the 3D, but it was just to get a 3D scene

00:20:24.230 --> 00:20:29.210
drawing at high speed so you can turn around
in one second, you can just do a spin.

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:34.180
And that took a lot of computer knowledge
of the hardware of the computer.

00:20:34.180 --> 00:20:41.480
And also how to draw a scene not using all
of the 3D equations that you normally would

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:48.610
use to compute a 3D scene, but restricting
the way that you could move to reduce the

00:20:48.610 --> 00:20:56.480
amount of computation that you needed to do
to turn the scene into a scene that is visually

00:20:56.480 --> 00:21:03.510
complex in 3D, do that very quickly, by not
having to worry about certain angles that

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:07.960
you would see, or turn your head or roll forward
or whatever.

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:15.210
So it restricted you to only one plane of
turning, where you're just rotating your head

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:16.210
around.

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:19.480
You can't look up and you can't look down
and you can't tilt your head sideways.

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:24.809
So by reducing your motion that way, it reduced
the kind of computations that we needed to

00:21:24.809 --> 00:21:25.809
do.

00:21:25.809 --> 00:21:30.120
Tim Ferriss: So if I'm hearing you correctly,
and I may not just because of my limitations

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:35.260
as a Luddite, or maybe not a Luddite, but
more of a muggle in this capacity, but it

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:40.230
seems like you applied positive constraints,
a number of constraints, so that you could

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:50.580
use, let's call it elegant code, to dramatically
improve the speed of the player experience.

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:54.730
And I'd love for you to explain two things
for people listening.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:58.440
Number one, when you're saying "us," who is
"us?"

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:05.760
And why do you think you guys were able to
create this breakthrough instead of perhaps

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:06.760
other teams?

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:11.539
Or were other people simply not looking at
it the same way you were in terms of optimizing

00:22:11.539 --> 00:22:15.850
for speed and using that as the primary driver
behind the development?

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:24.880
John Romero: So "us" is id Software, which
is a company that I co-founded in 1991.

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:29.840
We started 1990, but we officially started
the company in 1991.

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:33.830
And that's me and John Carmack, Tom Hall,
and Adrian Carmack.

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:36.900
Adrian and John are not related.

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:40.289
And it was just us four that founded this
company.

00:22:40.289 --> 00:22:49.940
And in 1991, which is the first year that
we were officially in our own office basically

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:55.480
to make games full-time, all day long, at
our own company, which is a major achievement

00:22:55.480 --> 00:23:01.529
for us because like everybody, we have jobs,
we had day jobs, but we had created a game

00:23:01.529 --> 00:23:07.600
in our off time and it did really well and
allowed us to basically start the company

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:08.770
formally.

00:23:08.770 --> 00:23:13.840
We were doing side-scrolling games at that
time using a technique that no one had ever

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:15.300
used before on a PC.

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:21.980
It hadn't been discovered for nine years since
the introduction of the PC until John Carmack

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:27.710
figured out how to get pixel smooth scrolling
horizontally on a PC and it looked like a

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:28.710
Nintendo.

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:30.440
Tim Ferriss: Sorry to interrupt, could you
give an example, just for people listening,

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:32.160
who may not know the terminology?

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:37.230
I know it seems self-explanatory, but a side-scrolling
game, what would be a well-known side-scrolling

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:38.320
game so people can visualize?

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:40.840
John Romero: Yeah, Super Mario Bros. —
Tim Ferriss: Super Mario.

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:44.750
John Romero: — is a side-scrolling game,
and there were thousands of them throughout

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:51.980
the '80s, but on a computer, on a PC, and
the PC came out in August of 1981, on the

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:57.770
PC, no side-scrolling game could move a dot
a time horizontally.

00:23:57.770 --> 00:24:00.020
It always moved by eight pixels.

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:02.410
It just chunked across the screen.

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:05.230
So it was very chunky and blocky when it moved.

00:24:05.230 --> 00:24:13.340
And that's because even moving the whole screen
itself took a lot of time, but to move it

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:18.610
by a pixel was kind of unimaginable because
there was no way to control that at a high

00:24:18.610 --> 00:24:19.610
speed.

00:24:19.610 --> 00:24:25.500
So when John made the breakthrough of 1990
to figure out how to do that horizontal movement,

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:31.220
like a Nintendo, like Super Mario Bros. on
a Nintendo, how to do that on the PC.

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:36.490
That was basically the moment that id Software
was born because it was like, we are doing

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:37.660
this in our own company.

00:24:37.660 --> 00:24:39.730
Oh, my God, no one has ever seen this before.

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:41.750
I'm just blown away.

00:24:41.750 --> 00:24:49.320
And when we finally started the company, I
think it was half a year after that moment

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:58.210
when we started it, in 1991, we made 13 games
that year and we made them at high speed.

00:24:58.210 --> 00:25:02.980
A game took us two months, but we would make
two games at a time.

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:06.230
So we were so busy.

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:13.500
The first 3D game that we made was called
Hovertank One, and that was just to get our

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:21.210
feet wet in 3D because we knew that 3D is
really where games are going eventually.

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:26.390
Because at that point there were very few
3D games and they were very, very slow.

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:31.760
None of them made a wall look like there were
bricks on it or rocks or whatever it was.

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:36.940
It was always some kind of solid color or
some little pattern of dots, two different

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:42.170
colored dots to make it look like another
color that doesn't really exist.

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:44.700
It's called dithering when you do that.

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:47.059
But it wasn't really advanced.

00:25:47.059 --> 00:25:55.200
So our focus was to make the screen appear
like other games except make it so we can

00:25:55.200 --> 00:26:04.419
move through the level smoothly, not in chunks,
and to be able to turn in any direction and

00:26:04.419 --> 00:26:10.250
do that at a very high frame rate because
no one had ever done that before.

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:13.870
And that was the focus of Hovertank One and
that game was made in two months.

00:26:13.870 --> 00:26:18.350
And that was John's big breakthrough, was
just creating the architecture of that engine

00:26:18.350 --> 00:26:22.860
and getting the rendering for 3D going at
that speed.

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:26.720
Tim Ferriss: 13 games in one year.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:32.470
I know nothing about game development, but
I imagine with a relatively small crew, that

00:26:32.470 --> 00:26:34.900
sounds like a lot of games.

00:26:34.900 --> 00:26:41.789
What do you think enabled you guys to do that
that maybe was not present for other people

00:26:41.789 --> 00:26:45.159
working on games who had much lower output?

00:26:45.159 --> 00:26:49.590
What were the ingredients or the approaches
or the principles, anything at all?

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:51.330
Maybe just tolerance for sleep deprivation.

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:52.330
I don't know.

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:55.270
What were the ingredients that allowed you
allowed you guys to do that?

00:26:55.270 --> 00:27:02.299
John Romero: So the way that we could achieve
this game development at such high speed back

00:27:02.299 --> 00:27:10.460
then was that when we got together as id Software,
we had already individually, other than Adrian,

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:16.660
who just learned how to put pixels on a computer
screen, but he was a fine artist on paper

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:25.090
already for his whole life, each one of us
four had 10 years of game development experience

00:27:25.090 --> 00:27:27.760
like all the time.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:31.929
And so we'd already made dozens of games each.

00:27:31.929 --> 00:27:37.289
Tom Hall, who was our creative director, he
was a programmer, an assembly language programmer.

00:27:37.289 --> 00:27:39.679
He made tons of adventure games.

00:27:39.679 --> 00:27:43.169
He did action arcade games in assembly language.

00:27:43.169 --> 00:27:46.070
So he was already a very fluent programmer.

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:48.830
John Carmack, of course, coded since he was
a kid.

00:27:48.830 --> 00:27:51.720
I coded since I was 11.

00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:57.700
So we'd been making games for 10 years and
the amount of games that we'd made was dozens

00:27:57.700 --> 00:27:58.700
each.

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:05.210
And when you make that many games that quickly,
you learn how to scope, and scoping is basically

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:11.950
defining exactly what that game should be
and not adding to it, subtracting from it

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:14.340
most likely if you're going to try and get
it done.

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:19.029
So scoping is like, this is the amount of
time that I have, this is probably the kind

00:28:19.029 --> 00:28:21.159
of game that we can make and how big we can
make it.

00:28:21.159 --> 00:28:22.190
Let's do that.

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:27.140
And as you get closer and closer to that deadline,
you start cutting things away that you don't

00:28:27.140 --> 00:28:29.200
need to hit that deadline.

00:28:29.200 --> 00:28:36.230
So when we did it before meeting each other,
when we did it on our own time, we set ourselves

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:41.250
our own goals for finishing our own games,
and we got really good at scoping.

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:47.070
So when we got together and we saw what we
could do together, we also scoped together

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:51.340
how big those games should be, and we had
to deliver within two months.

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:57.630
So we had to know how good every one of us
is, how fast we can all work, and then scope

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:00.250
a game to that limitation.

00:29:00.250 --> 00:29:06.530
And because we had 10 years of game dev experience,
we could just slam games out super fast, come

00:29:06.530 --> 00:29:12.510
up with ideas, bam, start from doing the graphics,
doing the animation, programming the engine,

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:16.779
making the levels, making the level editors
to create levels, you name it, all the tools

00:29:16.779 --> 00:29:22.909
that are in the background that no one sees,
to build the game data and put it together

00:29:22.909 --> 00:29:26.570
in packages that the game can access.

00:29:26.570 --> 00:29:32.030
There's so much technology that goes on before
you get to gameplay programming, but we were

00:29:32.030 --> 00:29:34.250
used to doing that already for 10 years.

00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:41.990
So it was not just the experience that we
had, but it was also the focus that we had.

00:29:41.990 --> 00:29:49.800
We were just so happy to be able to make games
all day long as a job, which is really just

00:29:49.800 --> 00:29:53.600
like the luckiest position ever.

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:55.890
So we could focus on doing that.

00:29:55.890 --> 00:30:00.280
We could scope our games down really, really
well.

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:05.760
We knew what to cut when it was time to start
cutting stuff if we're getting closer to our

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:06.760
deadline.

00:30:06.760 --> 00:30:14.370
And because there was no internet back then
and no one had cell phones, there was complete

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:16.840
and total focus because our phone never rang.

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:19.490
There was a phone on the wall and that thing
never rang.

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:21.390
So there's no interruptions.

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:27.380
Nobody's coming to the house and knocking
on the door in interrupting our thoughts.

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:34.450
So we could just focus for 12 hours a day
at least, and just code, design, you name

00:30:34.450 --> 00:30:36.940
it, constantly.

00:30:36.940 --> 00:30:43.890
Tim Ferriss: So to build on the emphasis you
put on scoping, which I think is certainly

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:47.899
applicable to so many different projects,
maybe all projects, that certainly applies

00:30:47.899 --> 00:30:53.570
to book projects and so on that I've been
involved with, podcasts, thematically choosing

00:30:53.570 --> 00:30:57.049
where you'll go and where you will not go.

00:30:57.049 --> 00:31:07.960
I was doing some research, and I read a discussion
of different, I suppose, principles at id

00:31:07.960 --> 00:31:15.120
Software, and a comment came up, which was
"No prototypes," which was attributed to you,

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:19.990
and talks about you guys being your own best
testing team and so on.

00:31:19.990 --> 00:31:24.740
Can't believe everything you read on the internet,
of course, but if this is true, if you could

00:31:24.740 --> 00:31:27.370
elaborate on what you mean by that.

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:33.159
John Romero: So to get that many games done
that fast, you can't be playing around with

00:31:33.159 --> 00:31:34.909
making prototypes.

00:31:34.909 --> 00:31:39.009
Prototypes are like, let's test this and see
if it works, and let's test that and see it

00:31:39.009 --> 00:31:41.320
— there's no testing.

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:44.720
It's like, here's the game that we're going
to make, here are all the characters that

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:49.000
go in the game, here's how many levels we
have, here are all the pieces of the levels

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:53.350
that need to be drawn, here's all the animations
that need to be created, all the tools that

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:54.750
need to be programmed, all that stuff.

00:31:54.750 --> 00:31:58.710
There's no way to do any prototyping if you're
going to get done in two months with four

00:31:58.710 --> 00:31:59.970
people.

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:01.730
So we had no prototypes.

00:32:01.730 --> 00:32:03.390
We knew how to code already.

00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:10.460
So because we can just see it in our minds,
we can just visualize it, and we communicated

00:32:10.460 --> 00:32:13.899
with each other just going, okay, it's going
to look like this on the screen.

00:32:13.899 --> 00:32:15.750
We're going to see mountains in the background.

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:19.020
On the foreground, we're going to have another
plane and we're going to have trees there,

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:22.920
and up in the trees we're going to have these
characters and their AI is going to be limited

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:23.920
to blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:28.840
And so we just kind of describe what the game
is, and we can do that within just like, let's

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:30.679
say an hour.

00:32:30.679 --> 00:32:38.730
At the very beginning of the new game, in
one hour we can describe that game extensively

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:42.690
for what we're going to create in the next
two months.

00:32:42.690 --> 00:32:47.649
There wasn't like, well, then we have to figure
out this.

00:32:47.649 --> 00:32:50.350
It was like, there's no figuring it out.

00:32:50.350 --> 00:32:52.899
We're just making it.

00:32:52.899 --> 00:32:54.620
So there weren't prototypes.

00:32:54.620 --> 00:32:56.929
It was just, we're making the game.

00:32:56.929 --> 00:33:04.510
Later on with bigger games that we could spend
more time on, we could do R&amp;D, but we still

00:33:04.510 --> 00:33:05.510
knew what we were making.

00:33:05.510 --> 00:33:06.510
We just needed to figure out how to make that
part of it.

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:07.510
But it wasn't like we built a piece of the
game and then throw it away.

00:33:07.510 --> 00:33:08.510
It was like we would build this functionality
in because that was the original design, and

00:33:08.510 --> 00:33:09.510
if we found out that that functionality actually
is a detriment to the game itself or the soul

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:10.510
of the game, then we would remove it.

00:33:10.510 --> 00:33:11.510
But it wasn't like we were prototyping it.

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:12.510
We were making that piece of technology in
the game.

00:33:12.510 --> 00:33:13.510
It was going to be part of the game, but we
find out that that is not where the game is

00:33:13.510 --> 00:33:14.510
really.

00:33:14.510 --> 00:33:15.510
And then we pull it out.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:16.510
But we didn't prototype.

00:33:16.510 --> 00:33:17.510
Tim Ferriss: So let me ask, I'm sure, a naive
question, but if you are going to ensure that,

00:33:17.510 --> 00:33:20.920
for instance, you can take an element like
that and remove it later if it is not contributing

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:26.830
to it or reinforcing the soul of the game,
do you need to think about that design spec

00:33:26.830 --> 00:33:34.260
in advance so you don't end up with a piece
you want to remove, but that is interdependent

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:36.990
with other elements in the game?

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:41.210
In other words, does the architecture, how
you approach it, need to be decided in advance

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:45.110
so that you have the capability of removing
something in that way?

00:33:45.110 --> 00:33:49.320
John Romero: When we're coming up with the
design of the game, let's say in this case

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:57.270
it's Wolfenstein 3D — and the inspiration
for Wolfenstein 3D was a 1981 game called

00:33:57.270 --> 00:33:58.860
Castle Wolfenstein.

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:04.610
And it had a lot of really great gameplay
features in it that we wanted to put in Wolfenstein

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:10.609
3D, such as when you shoot a guard, you could
drag them and hide them around a wall.

00:34:10.609 --> 00:34:16.419
You could search their bodies, you could go
up to storage chests and try and open them

00:34:16.419 --> 00:34:18.169
and maybe shoot them open.

00:34:18.169 --> 00:34:21.230
But that makes noise and might call the guards.

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:27.720
All these little pieces of design that we
wanted to replicate but replicate it in 3D

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:32.030
because Castle Wolfenstein was just a top-down
game from 1981.

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:37.770
It wasn't 3D, the screen didn't move around,
it was just like characters are marching around

00:34:37.770 --> 00:34:40.270
on the screen and that's it.

00:34:40.270 --> 00:34:44.480
They might leave the screen and disappear,
but your screen was static like many games

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:47.320
back in the '80s.

00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:53.070
When we were making Wolfenstein 3D, we start
programming in the dead body, dragging and

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:57.250
the searching, and we started doing that.

00:34:57.250 --> 00:34:58.460
That was the design.

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:00.320
So we already designed that we're doing it.

00:35:00.320 --> 00:35:03.690
We already got the code in to do it, and we're
now doing it.

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:08.820
We're playing the game and we're shooting
somebody, going up and searching them.

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:14.750
And as we're playing the game, it feels like
that doesn't match now what this game feels

00:35:14.750 --> 00:35:16.859
like, because the original game, it was perfect.

00:35:16.859 --> 00:35:23.150
It matched perfectly with the way that you
moved around and the feeling of the original

00:35:23.150 --> 00:35:24.940
game was more of a stealth game.

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:28.510
So Castle Wolfenstein was really the first
stealth game made.

00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:33.530
It was an action arcade game, but it was really
primarily about stealthing around and secretly

00:35:33.530 --> 00:35:36.339
getting out of this castle and escaping it.

00:35:36.339 --> 00:35:38.380
And we were doing that in our game as well.

00:35:38.380 --> 00:35:39.510
It was a recreation.

00:35:39.510 --> 00:35:43.460
But when we got to the point where we were
doing some of the similar actions that were

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:48.640
not just run and gun, we knew that that is
not what this game's strength is.

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:54.220
The strength of this game is doing this never
before seen high speed rendering and high

00:35:54.220 --> 00:35:57.140
speed movement through this maze.

00:35:57.140 --> 00:35:59.410
And no one had seen that before.

00:35:59.410 --> 00:36:05.540
And slowing you down and making the players
stop to do something else was anti the focus

00:36:05.540 --> 00:36:06.540
of the game.

00:36:06.540 --> 00:36:09.840
So the focus became this is the soul of the
game.

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:13.210
The soul of this game is high speed, run and
gun.

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:16.120
Anything that stops you needs to come out.

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:19.990
So even though we programmed in that stuff,
we pulled it out because it wasn't true to

00:36:19.990 --> 00:36:22.609
the new spirit of the game, the one that we
were making.

00:36:22.609 --> 00:36:29.819
It was like the original game's idea was transferred
to our game, but all of the details of those

00:36:29.819 --> 00:36:31.589
two games are different.

00:36:31.589 --> 00:36:36.210
The way that you shoot enemies in the original
Castle Wolfenstein was not the way that you

00:36:36.210 --> 00:36:37.210
do it in our game.

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:42.980
Our game is much more fluid and simple and
easy to control and very, very fast.

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:48.610
And so when we started focusing our design
in that part of it, we started removing everything

00:36:48.610 --> 00:36:50.680
that took away from that speed.

00:36:50.680 --> 00:36:55.660
But there were some things that we had to
leave in, like to open a door and wait for

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:59.340
the door to open, that's actually suspense
that we need in the game.

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:03.310
And if we didn't have any doorways, you're
just running through a maze just blowing everything

00:37:03.310 --> 00:37:12.859
away constantly nonstop, but you have no suspense
really.

00:37:12.859 --> 00:37:17.210
One of the original games' focuses was the
suspense of wondering if you'd made a noise

00:37:17.210 --> 00:37:22.380
and alerted the guards to come after you,
or if they heard you trying to open a chest

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:26.440
in the next room and you can see them in the
next room and you're just trying to open a

00:37:26.440 --> 00:37:31.030
chest and if you shoot it, then they're coming
in the room and they're going to try and arrest

00:37:31.030 --> 00:37:32.030
you.

00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:37.760
So we had a new focus of our design and that's
why we start removing and changing it.

00:37:37.760 --> 00:37:42.570
Tim Ferriss: So let me build on Wolfenstein
3D, and I have a question from a friend of

00:37:42.570 --> 00:37:46.520
mine because I mentioned to him that we were
going to be speaking, his name is Elan Lee,

00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:53.619
he's the co-founder, co-creator of Exploding
Kittens, not just the single game, but the

00:37:53.619 --> 00:37:58.230
company that produces many, many tabletop
games and card games.

00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:00.619
And he said, "Holy crap, amazing.

00:38:00.619 --> 00:38:04.030
Ask him how the pressure of promising the
world a game when he didn't have one somehow

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:08.120
cajoled the cells of his body to do the impossible
and will one into existence?"

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:13.760
And we went back and forth a little bit and
he said, "Let me know if you needed any links

00:38:13.760 --> 00:38:15.340
to early Romero development stories.

00:38:15.340 --> 00:38:17.280
That guy literally did the impossible."

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:24.170
So could you expand on what he means by promising
the world a game when he didn't have one,

00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:26.470
and how that cajoled the cells in your body?

00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:31.300
That may not be the right way to put it, but
to do the impossible, just for people who

00:38:31.300 --> 00:38:33.440
don't have any of that background.

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:37.230
John Romero: We made Wolfenstein 3D.

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:41.590
For me personally, that was my 87th game that
I'd made.

00:38:41.590 --> 00:38:44.250
Right after Wolfenstein 3D, we made Spear
of Destiny.

00:38:44.250 --> 00:38:45.390
That took us two months.

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:49.880
That was number 88.

00:38:49.880 --> 00:38:56.420
There was a game called ShadowCaster that
used our technology that we had made.

00:38:56.420 --> 00:39:03.020
So all the tools that I wrote, all any gameplay
code, menu code, all the stuff, we licensed

00:39:03.020 --> 00:39:06.099
our technology and give it to another company
and then they make a game.

00:39:06.099 --> 00:39:10.520
That's game number 89 called ShadowCaster.

00:39:10.520 --> 00:39:16.220
And then game number 90, not that I even was
counting back then, it was just like, next

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:18.050
game, we're going to make the next game.

00:39:18.050 --> 00:39:23.770
At the time, at very end of 1992, after making
all of these games, and really when Wolfenstein

00:39:23.770 --> 00:39:29.650
came out, everything changed because now we
have high-speed 3D.

00:39:29.650 --> 00:39:31.510
No one had ever seen this before.

00:39:31.510 --> 00:39:38.711
Ultima Underworld was out, and it was a really
great roleplaying game set in 3D, but the

00:39:38.711 --> 00:39:43.740
3D let you turn your head and look up and
down and all that stuff, which really slowed

00:39:43.740 --> 00:39:45.200
the game down.

00:39:45.200 --> 00:39:47.420
So it wasn't the high-speed game.

00:39:47.420 --> 00:39:50.839
You would step slowly through a dungeon, but
it was 3D.

00:39:50.839 --> 00:39:53.870
3D is now happening at this point.

00:39:53.870 --> 00:40:00.230
If it's not ours, it's probably slow, but
it is 3D because the world is now going to

00:40:00.230 --> 00:40:04.710
become 3D, but it's hard to get to that point.

00:40:04.710 --> 00:40:05.760
Our focus was speed.

00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:08.390
That was where we wanted to live.

00:40:08.390 --> 00:40:17.310
So with the next game, we had had such massive
success with Wolfenstein and Spear of Destiny

00:40:17.310 --> 00:40:22.380
that we jumped basically to the front of the
industry with those games.

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:28.970
Immediately, bam, we are now in the lead because
nobody was making this, and this was the new

00:40:28.970 --> 00:40:30.490
thing.

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:37.160
High speed 3D is the future, we're there first
and we need to keep our lead.

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:42.109
The planning for our next game after that,
and our planning was happening at the end

00:40:42.109 --> 00:40:52.760
of 1992, was the creation of the engine that
was a Wolfenstein-plus engine after Spear

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:53.760
of Destiny.

00:40:53.760 --> 00:41:00.060
It led to ShadowCaster, but that engine's
architecture was too limiting for what we

00:41:00.060 --> 00:41:02.319
had in mind for our next game.

00:41:02.319 --> 00:41:07.600
So that's why we licensed that technology
out and decided we are not using that 3D engine

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:09.730
architecture anymore.

00:41:09.730 --> 00:41:15.220
And so at that point, we'd made four 3D games,
four games in full 3D, high speed, all that.

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:23.440
For our fifth game we needed a new architecture
and to come up with a list of all of the things

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:30.310
that this new game would do, and we were thinking,
we need to go beyond anything we've ever seen.

00:41:30.310 --> 00:41:36.630
And this was the only time we ever did this,
the next game needs to be the best game we

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:41.569
could imagine playing, that there can't be
a better game in the world than this game.

00:41:41.569 --> 00:41:43.579
What would that game have?

00:41:43.579 --> 00:41:49.500
So we made a list, a bullet point list of
all the things that this new game should have.

00:41:49.500 --> 00:41:55.670
Obviously it needs to have light and dark,
it has to have light levels, it needs to be

00:41:55.670 --> 00:42:01.950
spooky, it needs to have bright areas, which
we had no lighting in Wolfenstein.

00:42:01.950 --> 00:42:04.250
It was just full bright.

00:42:04.250 --> 00:42:05.590
The lights were always on.

00:42:05.590 --> 00:42:08.220
So we need lighting control.

00:42:08.220 --> 00:42:09.220
We need height control.

00:42:09.220 --> 00:42:14.600
We need to be able to go up and down stairs,
we need to have giant tall rooms, really cramped

00:42:14.600 --> 00:42:15.600
rooms.

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:22.530
We need walls to be drawn at any angle, which
no game in history had ever done at that point

00:42:22.530 --> 00:42:28.359
where you can have a wall that's at an angle
in a wall that's jagged and all that.

00:42:28.359 --> 00:42:34.000
Every game before this next game was basically
a maze game.

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:40.130
And when you look at a maze from above, it's
just 90 degree wall curve turns.

00:42:40.130 --> 00:42:44.790
It's like those classic mazes, labyrinths,
where you're just drawing a line through the

00:42:44.790 --> 00:42:45.820
maze.

00:42:45.820 --> 00:42:50.790
That's what every single game from the '70s
until that point looked like.

00:42:50.790 --> 00:42:54.309
Wolfenstein 3D was a high speed maze game.

00:42:54.309 --> 00:42:55.450
It was the same thing.

00:42:55.450 --> 00:42:58.030
90 degree corners all over the place.

00:42:58.030 --> 00:43:02.090
So this next game would break that barrier.

00:43:02.090 --> 00:43:07.970
So we're going to have what we call non-orthogonal
walls that could create a space that no one

00:43:07.970 --> 00:43:09.490
had seen before.

00:43:09.490 --> 00:43:16.550
We were going to make this game multiplayer
so people could play with each other and against

00:43:16.550 --> 00:43:25.369
each other for the first time because right
then, local area networks, they were just

00:43:25.369 --> 00:43:30.840
barely starting to sell network cards and
the cables and all that stuff in all the computer

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:32.480
retail stores.

00:43:32.480 --> 00:43:36.410
So we would make a game that would take advantage
of a network if you ever had it, and it would

00:43:36.410 --> 00:43:41.590
take advantage of the modem to play against
or with your friends.

00:43:41.590 --> 00:43:46.640
And then we would also make the game open
so people could change the game to do whatever

00:43:46.640 --> 00:43:49.900
they wanted and make it their own, basically.

00:43:49.900 --> 00:43:53.640
They could make levels, they could make new
sounds, new graphics.

00:43:53.640 --> 00:43:57.200
They could change the whole game basically
and mod it.

00:43:57.200 --> 00:43:59.570
It's called mods, modification.

00:43:59.570 --> 00:44:04.309
So we would open it up to modders who wanted
to create a new experience using the tech

00:44:04.309 --> 00:44:10.440
that we created, and we would also give away
the first piece of this game for free into

00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:13.330
the world, and you don't have to pay for it.

00:44:13.330 --> 00:44:17.670
You just get it, and if you like it, you had
fun, if you want more, then you buy it from

00:44:17.670 --> 00:44:18.670
us.

00:44:18.670 --> 00:44:20.849
That's called shareware.

00:44:20.849 --> 00:44:22.109
So we did all of these.

00:44:22.109 --> 00:44:27.400
We had this whole list of everything we're
going to do, and we were very confident that

00:44:27.400 --> 00:44:32.650
we could do it because John was already planning
the architecture of the engine, he already

00:44:32.650 --> 00:44:36.319
was very confident in how he was going to
be able to do that.

00:44:36.319 --> 00:44:38.589
And at that point, we were just on top of
the world.

00:44:38.589 --> 00:44:43.060
We had just conquered everything with Wolfenstein
3D and with Spear of Destiny.

00:44:43.060 --> 00:44:47.470
And so we're just invincible feeling.

00:44:47.470 --> 00:44:56.119
So what we did was we took our two-week vacation
at the end of 1992, and we came back in January

00:44:56.119 --> 00:45:01.210
of '93 at the beginning of the year, and we're
going to start working on this new game that

00:45:01.210 --> 00:45:03.359
we called DOOM.

00:45:03.359 --> 00:45:11.480
And we decided for some reason that we were
going to put out a press release and tell

00:45:11.480 --> 00:45:17.630
everybody what's going to be in this game,
and it will be the greatest game that Earth

00:45:17.630 --> 00:45:19.230
has seen.

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:21.640
And we did.

00:45:21.640 --> 00:45:25.110
We put a press release out before we even
start writing the game.

00:45:25.110 --> 00:45:29.330
We put a press release out saying, this will
be the best game anyone has ever played on

00:45:29.330 --> 00:45:35.690
a PC, and this is what's going to be in it,
and it's a breakthrough in game technology

00:45:35.690 --> 00:45:38.460
and all this crazy stuff.

00:45:38.460 --> 00:45:41.660
We put a press release out saying that.

00:45:41.660 --> 00:45:43.540
So we needed to fulfill that.

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:45.250
And so we started working on it.

00:45:45.250 --> 00:45:46.280
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:47.310
John Romero: Crazy.

00:45:47.310 --> 00:45:48.310
Crazy.

00:45:48.310 --> 00:45:49.360
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's nuts.

00:45:49.360 --> 00:45:55.599
So there are, for instance, as thought exercises,
some companies that will write press releases

00:45:55.599 --> 00:46:04.320
in advance of developing a technology product
to help hone their focus on various elements,

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:06.140
but that is for internal use only.

00:46:06.140 --> 00:46:08.680
You guys actually put out the press release.

00:46:08.680 --> 00:46:12.570
What was the conversation around the press
release?

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:19.089
Because it's a smart team, there was some
thought, I'm sure, that went into that decision.

00:46:19.089 --> 00:46:23.480
What was the logic, if there was one behind
putting out the press release?

00:46:23.480 --> 00:46:30.420
John Romero: I don't know why we would've
put out a press release.

00:46:30.420 --> 00:46:31.710
It doesn't make any sense.

00:46:31.710 --> 00:46:37.570
But I think that what happened is Wolfenstein
3D came out — remember at this point, we

00:46:37.570 --> 00:46:40.849
were so used to making games extremely fast.

00:46:40.849 --> 00:46:46.080
With Wolfenstein, that was the very first
game that we could make in any amount of time

00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:47.130
that we wanted.

00:46:47.130 --> 00:46:49.950
There was no deadline on Wolfenstein.

00:46:49.950 --> 00:46:55.220
We made the shareware version of Wolfenstein
in four months and uploaded it.

00:46:55.220 --> 00:47:00.339
So there was four of us — well, mostly,
three of us — oh, no, sorry, there was four

00:47:00.339 --> 00:47:04.170
of us making Wolfenstein.

00:47:04.170 --> 00:47:10.370
We had Kevin Cloud in there for the very last
month to add graphics and do a bunch of other

00:47:10.370 --> 00:47:11.370
cool stuff.

00:47:11.370 --> 00:47:16.240
But we were very fast and we didn't have a
deadline on that game.

00:47:16.240 --> 00:47:19.230
And it released in May, the shareware.

00:47:19.230 --> 00:47:25.020
So we had the rest of 1992 just building up
this world domination of Wolfenstein.

00:47:25.020 --> 00:47:31.360
There was an internet top 100 list of games
every week, and Wolfenstein was at the top

00:47:31.360 --> 00:47:33.520
for the entire year after it came out.

00:47:33.520 --> 00:47:35.349
It was huge.

00:47:35.349 --> 00:47:40.800
So we were just on top of the world, but after
we released Spear of Destiny, it came out,

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:50.690
I believe in September of 1992, and no one
had heard anything from us in three months,

00:47:50.690 --> 00:47:54.980
which back then with these accelerated schedules
and people wanting to know what we're doing,

00:47:54.980 --> 00:47:57.910
we're finally now doing interviews with places.

00:47:57.910 --> 00:47:59.260
We're doing radio interviews.

00:47:59.260 --> 00:48:05.540
We have the local TV station in our office
interviewing us and recording our screens.

00:48:05.540 --> 00:48:08.329
We have them interviewing our publishers.

00:48:08.329 --> 00:48:12.030
People wanted all kinds of information about
what we were doing.

00:48:12.030 --> 00:48:15.980
So I think at that time it was like, let's
put out a press release telling people what

00:48:15.980 --> 00:48:21.130
we're going to start working on, because everyone
is asking us constantly what we're doing next.

00:48:21.130 --> 00:48:23.070
So let's just do a press release.

00:48:23.070 --> 00:48:25.730
And it's equal information for everyone.

00:48:25.730 --> 00:48:30.200
It's not like there's an exclusive with this
one computer magazine, Computer Gaming World

00:48:30.200 --> 00:48:31.200
or something.

00:48:31.200 --> 00:48:32.200
There was no exclusive.

00:48:32.200 --> 00:48:36.849
It was, "Here, everybody, this is what we're
working on, and now we have to dive back down

00:48:36.849 --> 00:48:38.540
in the ocean and start making it.

00:48:38.540 --> 00:48:40.340
Don't bug us."

00:48:40.340 --> 00:48:44.210
So I think the press release was probably
to let everyone know what we're going to do

00:48:44.210 --> 00:48:45.210
next.

00:48:45.210 --> 00:48:47.230
Tim Ferriss: That's incredible.

00:48:47.230 --> 00:48:48.440
All right.

00:48:48.440 --> 00:48:55.400
So I want to take a pause here just for a
second to ask you about — tell me if I'm

00:48:55.400 --> 00:48:56.760
pronouncing this correctly.

00:48:56.760 --> 00:48:57.760
Hyperthymesia?

00:48:57.760 --> 00:49:00.200
Am I getting that right?

00:49:00.200 --> 00:49:01.299
John Romero: Yeah, that's right.

00:49:01.299 --> 00:49:02.299
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:49:02.299 --> 00:49:04.819
So could you just define this for folks?

00:49:04.819 --> 00:49:10.400
And my question is also to what extent this
has been an asset and to what extent this

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:16.900
has had maybe a shadow side or been detrimental
in any way?

00:49:16.900 --> 00:49:20.369
So if you could just explain what it is and
then hop into that.

00:49:20.369 --> 00:49:26.460
John Romero: Yeah, hyperthymesia is a memory
disorder where the person with hyperthymesia

00:49:26.460 --> 00:49:29.920
has a superior autobiographical recall.

00:49:29.920 --> 00:49:40.960
And it ranges from really good recall of significant
events and things that happened in your life

00:49:40.960 --> 00:49:45.599
that most people would forget over time.

00:49:45.599 --> 00:49:47.940
With hyperthymesia, you never forget those
things.

00:49:47.940 --> 00:49:51.080
They just don't go away.

00:49:51.080 --> 00:49:55.619
You wouldn't know it if you're young, but
as you get older and you start to lose decades

00:49:55.619 --> 00:50:00.589
of memories because you don't use them and
they're not significant anymore, hyperthymesia,

00:50:00.589 --> 00:50:01.589
you don't lose them.

00:50:01.589 --> 00:50:03.960
They're always there.

00:50:03.960 --> 00:50:11.079
And the extreme end of hyperthymesia is where
you remember every second of your life since

00:50:11.079 --> 00:50:20.530
you got hyperthymesia, and it could be born
with, but sometimes it is an induced memory

00:50:20.530 --> 00:50:21.880
disorder.

00:50:21.880 --> 00:50:28.760
So people have been hit in the head with a
baseball and boom, they have hyperthymesia

00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:36.599
and they record every second of their life,
which gets to be kind of insane because everything

00:50:36.599 --> 00:50:39.630
that you've ever done is always present in
your brain.

00:50:39.630 --> 00:50:47.940
And having all of these thoughts, all of these
memories constantly, it's a big hindrance,

00:50:47.940 --> 00:50:53.250
I guess, in just normal operation like just
living.

00:50:53.250 --> 00:51:00.510
My hyperthymesia is really — I remember
basically everything that has happened to

00:51:00.510 --> 00:51:07.119
me, mostly all the significant stuff and things
that are connected to it.

00:51:07.119 --> 00:51:15.329
So like music and movies and programming and
games and that kind of stuff, I would remember

00:51:15.329 --> 00:51:18.530
basically all of that stuff through all the
years.

00:51:18.530 --> 00:51:21.359
And things that I cared about, I would remember
those things.

00:51:21.359 --> 00:51:28.750
I would remember the names of people who wrote
games, when those games were published, the

00:51:28.750 --> 00:51:31.480
year that they were published, all of that
stuff.

00:51:31.480 --> 00:51:34.020
So I would remember everything.

00:51:34.020 --> 00:51:39.960
So it makes it a lot easier to just say, "Hey,
what happened in 1983 when you were doing

00:51:39.960 --> 00:51:40.960
blah, blah, blah?"

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:43.859
And I was like, "Well, I was actually programming
this stuff, but I didn't have my computer,

00:51:43.859 --> 00:51:48.670
so I had to do it on paper in the kitchen
and [inaudible]."

00:51:48.670 --> 00:51:50.760
I could tell people what I did whenever.

00:51:50.760 --> 00:51:54.150
Tim Ferriss: Have you had that, to the best
of your knowledge, since birth or was that

00:51:54.150 --> 00:51:55.690
induced at some point?

00:51:55.690 --> 00:52:02.829
John Romero: I think I've had it mostly since
birth, but it got stronger when I started

00:52:02.829 --> 00:52:09.109
to learn how to code at really high speed
after getting the computer in the house.

00:52:09.109 --> 00:52:11.859
I was learning so much, so fast.

00:52:11.859 --> 00:52:16.550
I actually had to take breaks from learning
because my head was just packed full of stuff

00:52:16.550 --> 00:52:22.671
and it needed to settle, and I had to stop
myself from learning for a day or two before

00:52:22.671 --> 00:52:26.819
I could jump back in and fill my head full
of stuff again.

00:52:26.819 --> 00:52:33.579
And due to the nature of what I was learning
in the early '80s, which is learning every

00:52:33.579 --> 00:52:40.220
byte of memory in an entire computer, because
back then, the knowledge of language wasn't

00:52:40.220 --> 00:52:43.819
as important as the knowledge of hardware
at that time.

00:52:43.819 --> 00:52:47.460
So I needed to learn all of the hardware of
the computer that I was programming and then

00:52:47.460 --> 00:52:53.520
the languages on top of that, and then start
creating off of the top of that information.

00:52:53.520 --> 00:53:00.750
So that was also forcing function to induce
memory of everything basically that was happening.

00:53:00.750 --> 00:53:06.270
Tim Ferriss: I have one family member who
has, almost certainly, I never had heard this

00:53:06.270 --> 00:53:08.910
term before, but hyperthymesia.

00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:18.619
He can recall just about every fact, every
experience, significant or trivial, from his

00:53:18.619 --> 00:53:19.619
past.

00:53:19.619 --> 00:53:25.619
So he's much older than I am but can recall
the seating arrangement in every class that

00:53:25.619 --> 00:53:31.380
he was ever in, every word of every foreign
language he's ever studied, et cetera.

00:53:31.380 --> 00:53:40.030
And what I've seen is that, that is a superpower
on so many levels but also, as an example,

00:53:40.030 --> 00:53:49.049
has been challenging for him because negative
experiences, arguments, let's just say slight

00:53:49.049 --> 00:53:56.730
moral injuries, all of these things are also
as ever present for him and it's very challenging.

00:53:56.730 --> 00:54:03.960
And I'm wondering if you have seen any, I
don't want to say trade-offs, but challenges

00:54:03.960 --> 00:54:08.079
related to having such an enhanced memory
besides what you've mentioned.

00:54:08.079 --> 00:54:13.150
John Romero: I'd say that's totally true for
a lot of people that have that.

00:54:13.150 --> 00:54:22.710
For me, a lot of it is that the, say, negative/hindrance
would be that, I think a lot of things are

00:54:22.710 --> 00:54:25.730
similar in importance even if they aren't.

00:54:25.730 --> 00:54:32.760
So I would think that this possibly insignificant
detail is more important than anyone else

00:54:32.760 --> 00:54:33.760
would think.

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:37.150
And the reason why I think it is because the
way it's connected to other things.

00:54:37.150 --> 00:54:41.940
It's like if that didn't happen then these
other things wouldn't have happened as well,

00:54:41.940 --> 00:54:42.940
you know?

00:54:42.940 --> 00:54:43.940
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:54:43.940 --> 00:54:44.940
John Romero: Yeah.

00:54:44.940 --> 00:54:49.380
So it's like not knowing, well, say, for this
book, what's important and what isn't important.

00:54:49.380 --> 00:54:53.700
But it was really important, then I went to
the heavy metal concert with this person.

00:54:53.700 --> 00:54:59.910
Tim Ferriss: So I could see that being — has
that been challenging in group environments,

00:54:59.910 --> 00:55:04.650
team environments where you're trying to weigh
the relative significance but because, in

00:55:04.650 --> 00:55:11.130
your mind, some of these elements that might
not be deemed important by other people are

00:55:11.130 --> 00:55:15.290
sort of importance-adjacent, so you weigh
them more heavily?

00:55:15.290 --> 00:55:18.630
Has that been challenging in team environments
at all?

00:55:18.630 --> 00:55:25.520
John Romero: Not really, because I think the
most helpful part of that is just knowing

00:55:25.520 --> 00:55:33.810
what has been made before, what games and
game design techniques, programming techniques,

00:55:33.810 --> 00:55:37.800
approaches to certain control issues, like
that stuff.

00:55:37.800 --> 00:55:44.910
Having that and luckily just having played
so many games over so long a time, that has

00:55:44.910 --> 00:55:51.589
been really helpful to be able to recall solutions
or other approaches people have taken that

00:55:51.589 --> 00:55:55.210
we don't need to research or figure it out
again, reinvent the wheel.

00:55:55.210 --> 00:56:01.540
It's like this game in 1984 did this on an
Atari 800, and we might be able to do that

00:56:01.540 --> 00:56:03.410
on the PC or something like that.

00:56:03.410 --> 00:56:09.329
So it was more helpful to solving issues or
being able to make sure that we're not replicating

00:56:09.329 --> 00:56:16.410
failed designs of the past and that we were
always creating new things I hadn't seen before,

00:56:16.410 --> 00:56:17.410
which was really helpful.

00:56:17.410 --> 00:56:22.890
Tim Ferriss: How important, or how much of
a contributor do you think to the ongoing

00:56:22.890 --> 00:56:27.579
success of the team was having two games,
and therefore two options, for things to work

00:56:27.579 --> 00:56:29.559
on at any given point in time?

00:56:29.559 --> 00:56:40.200
John Romero: I would say that having two games
at a time was not — it did not impact really

00:56:40.200 --> 00:56:44.520
anything because we knew what each of the
games was, what we were doing on both those

00:56:44.520 --> 00:56:45.520
games.

00:56:45.520 --> 00:56:51.440
One of those games came out a month before
the other game, so that was the priority.

00:56:51.440 --> 00:56:59.119
But what we did was we divided our time into
two roughly eight-hour days or seven-hour

00:56:59.119 --> 00:57:00.119
days.

00:57:00.119 --> 00:57:05.760
So we would go from 10:00 to 6:00 on one game
and then 6:00 to 2:00 on the next game.

00:57:05.760 --> 00:57:10.460
So we would just all shift because it was
really important to be in sync with each other.

00:57:10.460 --> 00:57:16.039
When we needed to ask questions or whatever,
we were all on a project, and it wasn't like,

00:57:16.039 --> 00:57:17.589
"Oh, you're just making a level for the other
game.

00:57:17.589 --> 00:57:19.310
Well, I guess I can't ask you."

00:57:19.310 --> 00:57:21.630
It was like we were always in sync.

00:57:21.630 --> 00:57:24.619
So we would shift to the next game and start
working on that.

00:57:24.619 --> 00:57:27.390
And then the next day we start back on the
other game.

00:57:27.390 --> 00:57:34.270
And the reason why we're doing eight hours
and eight hours or seven and seven was because

00:57:34.270 --> 00:57:38.240
we love making games.

00:57:38.240 --> 00:57:39.740
We wanted to get these games done.

00:57:39.740 --> 00:57:40.910
We're learning a ton.

00:57:40.910 --> 00:57:44.370
It was super fun being together and making
these games.

00:57:44.370 --> 00:57:46.900
We'd be laughing.

00:57:46.900 --> 00:57:49.680
It would be new things that we're inventing.

00:57:49.680 --> 00:57:54.610
It was like being in a lab where things are
going right all the time.

00:57:54.610 --> 00:58:00.299
So it was super fun and we could do those
hours without a problem.

00:58:00.299 --> 00:58:02.960
And we did it for a whole year.

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:09.070
All of 1991 was spent with all the hours that
we were awake working on games, two games

00:58:09.070 --> 00:58:11.270
at a time at least.

00:58:11.270 --> 00:58:15.940
Because we were in sync at the same time when
we shifted projects, we didn't get creative

00:58:15.940 --> 00:58:17.099
block with something.

00:58:17.099 --> 00:58:19.360
It's like, "Ah, I can't even figure out what
I'm going to do at this level.

00:58:19.360 --> 00:58:21.220
I'm going to just make a level for the other
game."

00:58:21.220 --> 00:58:23.740
We would help each other if anyone got stuck.

00:58:23.740 --> 00:58:24.740
It's like, "You know what?

00:58:24.740 --> 00:58:26.480
I don't know what I'm going to do for this
one section."

00:58:26.480 --> 00:58:29.079
And I'm like, "Well, put a meat grinder in
there or something."

00:58:29.079 --> 00:58:31.559
And it's like, "Oh, yeah, good idea."

00:58:31.559 --> 00:58:33.390
So we would help each other.

00:58:33.390 --> 00:58:35.910
But most of the time we already knew what
we were doing.

00:58:35.910 --> 00:58:39.280
We kind of freethought everything out before
we started working on these games, and we

00:58:39.280 --> 00:58:41.360
could do it at really high speed.

00:58:41.360 --> 00:58:45.200
So we just executed because that was the really
important part.

00:58:45.200 --> 00:58:50.520
We had got the ideas, and we have lots of
experience, and it was all about the execution

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:53.510
of our ideas and in sync.

00:58:53.510 --> 00:58:58.490
And luckily, because we had such a small team
of four people, that could happen very fast.

00:58:58.490 --> 00:59:04.710
The bigger the team gets, the harder and longer
it takes to make one vision come out the right

00:59:04.710 --> 00:59:13.819
way, but with four people to have very distinct
jobs to do, it works really, really well.

00:59:13.819 --> 00:59:20.890
Tim Ferriss: DOOM, we paused at the press
release.

00:59:20.890 --> 00:59:24.670
You go on to create DOOM.

00:59:24.670 --> 00:59:31.410
When did you, after its release, or maybe
in the course of developing it, realize, "Holy

00:59:31.410 --> 00:59:34.670
shit, this is a monster or it's going to be
a monster.

00:59:34.670 --> 00:59:41.490
This is going to be bigger than even our grandest
press releases could have predicted."

00:59:41.490 --> 00:59:46.069
Was there a moment or an event, a day, anything,
that comes to mind?

00:59:46.069 --> 00:59:51.799
John Romero: Well, several things happened
during the development that were interesting

00:59:51.799 --> 00:59:53.730
in that respect.

00:59:53.730 --> 00:59:58.700
We knew at the very beginning of making the
game that it was going to be the greatest

00:59:58.700 --> 01:00:03.970
game that we've ever played because that was
our focus of — why we're making the game.

01:00:03.970 --> 01:00:11.480
So we already knew it was going to be awesome
and the best game that we had ever played.

01:00:11.480 --> 01:00:13.109
That was already a given.

01:00:13.109 --> 01:00:22.000
But while we're making the game, we got to
a point when Tom Hall left the team in August

01:00:22.000 --> 01:00:29.700
of 1993, and we brought in Sandy Petersen
to replace him doing level design work.

01:00:29.700 --> 01:00:35.030
And then I basically took over as the game
designer of the game.

01:00:35.030 --> 01:00:39.760
And that's how we started stripping a lot
of stuff out of the game like lives and score

01:00:39.760 --> 01:00:45.770
and getting rid of items that didn't matter,
really streamlining the design.

01:00:45.770 --> 01:00:54.710
Sandy was making levels, and at this point,
the design language for the level creation

01:00:54.710 --> 01:00:58.920
was figured out because I needed to solve
that problem with like, "What are our levels

01:00:58.920 --> 01:01:04.869
going to look like that maximizes the use
of our technology that no one has seen and

01:01:04.869 --> 01:01:11.339
creates spaces no one has ever experienced
before in a way that is kind of drastic and

01:01:11.339 --> 01:01:13.819
not a little bit better than Wolfenstein 3D?"

01:01:13.819 --> 01:01:17.390
It needed to be drastically better than Wolfenstein
3D.

01:01:17.390 --> 01:01:21.970
So that took some work to come up with this
abstract level design style.

01:01:21.970 --> 01:01:26.799
And when that happened and we started to make
stuff, it's like, "Okay.

01:01:26.799 --> 01:01:32.910
This is looking really great," Sandy Petersen
immediately started picking up that style

01:01:32.910 --> 01:01:36.650
because I would sit and talk to him at the
very beginning about — like, "Here's what

01:01:36.650 --> 01:01:40.380
is important about using this technology that
makes us stand out."

01:01:40.380 --> 01:01:44.200
And so he started putting those elements in
his levels.

01:01:44.200 --> 01:01:49.390
And when I got to run Sandy's levels, not
mine — because when I make my own levels,

01:01:49.390 --> 01:01:50.569
I know everything that's in it.

01:01:50.569 --> 01:01:56.380
So I'm just testing to make sure it looks
good, it's working well, the enemies are doing

01:01:56.380 --> 01:01:59.660
what I want to do, the pacing is correct.

01:01:59.660 --> 01:02:02.460
When combat happens is when I want it to happen.

01:02:02.460 --> 01:02:08.630
When exploration happens, I have the area
cleared for the player to do that, all of

01:02:08.630 --> 01:02:09.980
these things.

01:02:09.980 --> 01:02:14.420
But when I play Sandy's levels for the first
time, I know nothing about it.

01:02:14.420 --> 01:02:18.130
And I remember the first time when I started
playing his levels, I knew what people were

01:02:18.130 --> 01:02:19.130
going to feel like.

01:02:19.130 --> 01:02:20.130
Finally, right?

01:02:20.130 --> 01:02:21.130
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:02:21.130 --> 01:02:24.329
John Romero: And it was like, "Wow, this is
scary."

01:02:24.329 --> 01:02:29.540
I'm going through this level, and I'm hearing
these monsters moving around.

01:02:29.540 --> 01:02:32.880
I don't know where they're at, but I don't
like that they're coming for me somehow.

01:02:32.880 --> 01:02:40.520
And it was great because I finally got to
feel in a game design, feel the game experience

01:02:40.520 --> 01:02:44.430
way what people would feel when they're playing
it.

01:02:44.430 --> 01:02:49.309
Now, at that point, I'm so used to the technology
that it's like whatever.

01:02:49.309 --> 01:02:53.950
Even though it was like it changed everything,
no one had seen a screen that looked this

01:02:53.950 --> 01:02:58.200
way, for us, it was just like another day
at work, whatever.

01:02:58.200 --> 01:03:02.900
So I was just focused on the experience part
of it.

01:03:02.900 --> 01:03:08.309
So that to me was really fun to play a level
I didn't make and experience in a little way

01:03:08.309 --> 01:03:10.940
what people were going to feel like.

01:03:10.940 --> 01:03:17.520
And then we got to a point where it's early
October and I, for some reason, look at that

01:03:17.520 --> 01:03:21.069
press release again and look at the bullet
points because I'm just like as a producer

01:03:21.069 --> 01:03:24.670
type person, I need to make sure we're putting
everything in the game.

01:03:24.670 --> 01:03:29.609
We didn't promise stuff that we didn't deliver
on, and we need to mostly get that bullet

01:03:29.609 --> 01:03:30.760
point list done.

01:03:30.760 --> 01:03:33.230
And I saw that we were missing multiplayer.

01:03:33.230 --> 01:03:34.579
We didn't even think about it.

01:03:34.579 --> 01:03:39.800
We thought about it in January, but we get
to the point of the game where the game is

01:03:39.800 --> 01:03:42.770
mostly done now, and it's early October.

01:03:42.770 --> 01:03:44.930
And I'm like, "Whoa.

01:03:44.930 --> 01:03:45.930
Yeah.

01:03:45.930 --> 01:03:46.930
Whoo.

01:03:46.930 --> 01:03:47.930
There's no multiplayer."

01:03:47.930 --> 01:03:53.829
I go and I tell John, I'm like, "You will
not believe this.

01:03:53.829 --> 01:03:55.760
Yeah, there's no multiplayer.

01:03:55.760 --> 01:03:57.750
Remember, we're going to do that?"

01:03:57.750 --> 01:04:01.960
And he is like, "Oh, yeah."

01:04:01.960 --> 01:04:03.410
John's a genius.

01:04:03.410 --> 01:04:05.750
And so John figured out.

01:04:05.750 --> 01:04:11.130
He kind of immediately in his brain was just
like, "Oh, we'll just solve it with a peer-to-peer

01:04:11.130 --> 01:04:12.240
networking model."

01:04:12.240 --> 01:04:18.950
So he figured out how he's going to bolt multiplayer
onto the game and make it feel like it's been

01:04:18.950 --> 01:04:19.950
there the whole time.

01:04:19.950 --> 01:04:23.130
And so it just took him a few hours.

01:04:23.130 --> 01:04:28.230
He just needed to learn how to get network
packets going between computers over our network,

01:04:28.230 --> 01:04:30.109
and then how to get the game.

01:04:30.109 --> 01:04:31.990
What data does he send between computers?

01:04:31.990 --> 01:04:34.869
He's trying to minimize the amount of data
so it's fast.

01:04:34.869 --> 01:04:40.869
And he learned within just hours the techniques
that he needed to do.

01:04:40.869 --> 01:04:48.470
And it took a couple days to hammer out some
of the speed issues, but it was fast work

01:04:48.470 --> 01:04:49.470
before.

01:04:49.470 --> 01:04:50.640
I'd seen him shoot a character.

01:04:50.640 --> 01:04:56.030
He had two computers going and he's moving
a character in one screen, the other one,

01:04:56.030 --> 01:04:59.960
you see the guy running around inside the
level, it's like, "Oh, my God.

01:04:59.960 --> 01:05:03.760
We've never seen this before in any game."

01:05:03.760 --> 01:05:07.690
And then you shoot him and the guy dies, and
it's like, "Oh, my God, this is going to be

01:05:07.690 --> 01:05:10.780
the most fun thing in the world."

01:05:10.780 --> 01:05:16.211
And I go back into my office, and I get that
code running on my machine and we just start

01:05:16.211 --> 01:05:18.390
shooting each other, and it's like, "This
is nuts.

01:05:18.390 --> 01:05:19.619
Oh, my God."

01:05:19.619 --> 01:05:24.910
And then I'm imagining the level I'm currently
working on and seeing two people fighting

01:05:24.910 --> 01:05:27.450
in another room from my viewpoint.

01:05:27.450 --> 01:05:30.770
And I'm just like, "There's nothing like this
on Earth.

01:05:30.770 --> 01:05:33.970
Oh, my God, this is changing everything."

01:05:33.970 --> 01:05:36.319
So I got so excited at that point.

01:05:36.319 --> 01:05:43.190
So that was another like, "Holy shit, this
is going to be so different and blow everybody

01:05:43.190 --> 01:05:45.410
away and change everything."

01:05:45.410 --> 01:05:52.270
So multiplayer was — when I saw that, and
this is October, and so that was really amazing.

01:05:52.270 --> 01:05:57.990
And then the lead up to when we're about to
release the game, we're not online talking

01:05:57.990 --> 01:06:03.760
to anybody, but somehow people knew we were
getting close.

01:06:03.760 --> 01:06:08.869
We were starting to get random phone calls
at the office.

01:06:08.869 --> 01:06:18.390
There's these weird subcultures going on in
Usenet around DOOM and people creating games

01:06:18.390 --> 01:06:23.329
based on DOOM being the best game that could
ever be made.

01:06:23.329 --> 01:06:29.930
And then creating a really crude version of
that and calling it Smashing Pumpkins Into

01:06:29.930 --> 01:06:32.280
Small Piles Of Putrid Debris.

01:06:32.280 --> 01:06:37.140
And because that was such a funny thing, we
put it as a cheat code in DOOM.

01:06:37.140 --> 01:06:42.170
Just there was a lot of internet activity
around the fact that we're about to release

01:06:42.170 --> 01:06:45.609
the game, and we never said anything.

01:06:45.609 --> 01:06:51.220
So it was really weird that the world was
starting to go nuts before we launched the

01:06:51.220 --> 01:06:52.220
game.

01:06:52.220 --> 01:06:54.970
And that was the third like, "Okay."

01:06:54.970 --> 01:07:00.650
But up until that point, every game that we
made, we would put it out and then our plan

01:07:00.650 --> 01:07:02.480
is to just wipe that game out with the next
one.

01:07:02.480 --> 01:07:04.690
Like the next game, we're going to destroy
that thing.

01:07:04.690 --> 01:07:07.130
So it's like boom, game's out, next.

01:07:07.130 --> 01:07:14.670
So we never thought that DOOM was going to
be living actively for 30 years, which is

01:07:14.670 --> 01:07:17.130
this year, it'll be 30 years old.

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:21.559
Stuff is being made for the game on a daily
basis all the time.

01:07:21.559 --> 01:07:27.099
In one of the greatest mods that has been
created was released not that long ago, this

01:07:27.099 --> 01:07:28.099
year.

01:07:28.099 --> 01:07:29.099
Incredible.

01:07:29.099 --> 01:07:33.020
It's called MyHouse.WAD.

01:07:33.020 --> 01:07:35.540
People are still making insane stuff for the
game.

01:07:35.540 --> 01:07:38.460
But we never thought that was going to happen.

01:07:38.460 --> 01:07:41.130
We just thought we were going to make a really
great game and the next game's going to wipe

01:07:41.130 --> 01:07:42.130
it out.

01:07:42.130 --> 01:07:43.130
That was it.

01:07:43.130 --> 01:07:44.160
That was the goal.

01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:48.700
Tim Ferriss: Let's hop back to something that
you mentioned in passing that you left in

01:07:48.700 --> 01:07:49.920
1996.

01:07:49.920 --> 01:07:56.480
Could you tell the story of how that came
to be, how you ended up leaving the company?

01:07:56.480 --> 01:08:02.000
John Romero: We started making the last game
that I had worked on, at id Softwares named

01:08:02.000 --> 01:08:08.570
Quake, and we started working on Quake in
1995 as a team.

01:08:08.570 --> 01:08:12.799
John had already kind of started planning
the technology at the end of '94 when we were

01:08:12.799 --> 01:08:14.440
finishing DOOM II.

01:08:14.440 --> 01:08:20.650
He was exploring the technology for the next
game, which was going to be a massive, massive

01:08:20.650 --> 01:08:24.400
jump in technology.

01:08:24.400 --> 01:08:30.480
And so when we started as a team in 1995 to
make Quake, it was like making DOOM, it was

01:08:30.480 --> 01:08:34.150
like, here's this giant mountain we got to
climb again.

01:08:34.150 --> 01:08:40.120
There's a lot of tools, there's a lot of foundational
work that has to get done and let's do it.

01:08:40.120 --> 01:08:45.460
So we just started working on it, and the
design of the game also was like, remember

01:08:45.460 --> 01:08:50.230
we started making Quake and we couldn't because
the tech wasn't going to be there, but this

01:08:50.230 --> 01:08:55.839
now was going to be the tech that we could
use to make Quake, which is why we named it

01:08:55.839 --> 01:08:56.839
Quake.

01:08:56.839 --> 01:08:58.890
So it's like, we're going to do that game.

01:08:58.890 --> 01:09:04.790
That game was a medieval 3D adventure with
combat.

01:09:04.790 --> 01:09:09.170
It wasn't a first-person shooter because that
never existed at that time.

01:09:09.170 --> 01:09:12.989
It was going to be a 3D action, medieval.

01:09:12.989 --> 01:09:22.060
We were going to recreate our D&amp;D adventures
that we had and put it in full 3D, in a real,

01:09:22.060 --> 01:09:23.420
full-3D game.

01:09:23.420 --> 01:09:26.160
So that was the design.

01:09:26.160 --> 01:09:31.410
And as I am making the level editor and kind
of experimenting with 3D, there's a lot of

01:09:31.410 --> 01:09:37.750
other stuff going on at the company because
DOOM changed the planet and then DOOM II again

01:09:37.750 --> 01:09:40.990
was even better than the original DOOM.

01:09:40.990 --> 01:09:47.460
It was crazy because we only had at the time
when we shipped DOOM where it was only six

01:09:47.460 --> 01:09:55.790
of us and on top of that, we had three other
people in the company to handle business,

01:09:55.790 --> 01:10:00.650
all of the managerial type stuff and all of
the technical support type stuff.

01:10:00.650 --> 01:10:03.090
Tim Ferriss: That's incredible.

01:10:03.090 --> 01:10:04.640
It's just so wild.

01:10:04.640 --> 01:10:08.250
John Romero: It was a very small company.

01:10:08.250 --> 01:10:16.770
And working on Quake, we had gotten our team
up to 10 eventually to make Quake and still

01:10:16.770 --> 01:10:21.460
with the other three people I think on top
of that.

01:10:21.460 --> 01:10:24.960
The company was 13 people when Quake launched.

01:10:24.960 --> 01:10:32.660
So we had a very small team and we had the
world coming at us, and there was so much

01:10:32.660 --> 01:10:40.811
to deal with DOOM's impact and the articles,
the hint guides, the strategy guides, the

01:10:40.811 --> 01:10:46.770
other projects that we had going that it took
up a lot of time, which wasn't bad because

01:10:46.770 --> 01:10:52.150
at that time there was a lot of technology
creation for Quake going on.

01:10:52.150 --> 01:10:56.660
And I was creating the level editor for the
level design team to start experimenting with,

01:10:56.660 --> 01:11:03.060
and it was really just two other people on
the team doing level design in full 3D for

01:11:03.060 --> 01:11:04.060
the first time.

01:11:04.060 --> 01:11:08.400
And that was Sandy Petersen and American McGee.

01:11:08.400 --> 01:11:11.040
So it was still pretty small.

01:11:11.040 --> 01:11:15.900
We still had more people eventually to hire
onto the team to get us to 10 by the time

01:11:15.900 --> 01:11:16.900
Quake was shipped.

01:11:16.900 --> 01:11:19.350
But at this time there was probably seven
of us.

01:11:19.350 --> 01:11:21.120
It was pretty small.

01:11:21.120 --> 01:11:28.090
During that time the design that of that medieval
game kept getting stronger and stronger.

01:11:28.090 --> 01:11:34.159
It was getting reinforced with more things
that could be done to reinforce the feeling

01:11:34.159 --> 01:11:40.469
of that medieval world and do new things that
we hadn't seen a game do at that time in lots

01:11:40.469 --> 01:11:41.840
of different ways.

01:11:41.840 --> 01:11:47.390
So it was becoming a really strong design,
really strong, really cool.

01:11:47.390 --> 01:11:55.170
And the problem was that we were spending
all of that year kind of spinning our wheels,

01:11:55.170 --> 01:12:00.350
doing 3D levels, little pieces of levels and
stuff, but we didn't have a gameplay programmer

01:12:00.350 --> 01:12:05.090
that could actually do anything in the game
because the engine wasn't ready.

01:12:05.090 --> 01:12:08.940
We're waiting for that engine to actually
happen so we could start using it.

01:12:08.940 --> 01:12:10.560
Tim Ferriss: John, can I pause you for one
second?

01:12:10.560 --> 01:12:15.360
Just for people who hear this word but may
not understand how it applies within game

01:12:15.360 --> 01:12:19.219
development, could you just define engine?

01:12:19.219 --> 01:12:22.620
In simple terms, what is an engine?

01:12:22.620 --> 01:12:28.640
John Romero: So at this point in our game
development, we had created this term called

01:12:28.640 --> 01:12:29.640
engine.

01:12:29.640 --> 01:12:36.630
And the engine is what drives the game, and
that is the thing that puts the images up

01:12:36.630 --> 01:12:38.020
on the screen.

01:12:38.020 --> 01:12:40.909
It's the thing that loads files and saves
files.

01:12:40.909 --> 01:12:45.889
It's the thing that takes input in and makes
gameplay happen.

01:12:45.889 --> 01:12:51.010
It's what makes sound come out and music come
out of the game.

01:12:51.010 --> 01:12:59.080
And so the core functionality that is needed
for a game to run without any of the graphics

01:12:59.080 --> 01:13:03.719
and any of the actual sound effects themselves,
the core technology is called an engine.

01:13:03.719 --> 01:13:07.820
Because you can put the data in and it will
come out, it will play.

01:13:07.820 --> 01:13:12.800
You put sound effects in, and then you use
game in gameplay code, you define when those

01:13:12.800 --> 01:13:14.960
will play and they come out.

01:13:14.960 --> 01:13:22.830
And so it's an entire set of code that any
game can be made from.

01:13:22.830 --> 01:13:24.360
So it's called an engine.

01:13:24.360 --> 01:13:30.580
And we pioneered the idea of an engine in
1990 with our original Commander Keen game.

01:13:30.580 --> 01:13:34.580
We started calling it an engine even before
we launched Keen.

01:13:34.580 --> 01:13:36.340
So we start that idea in 1990.

01:13:36.340 --> 01:13:46.440
Now we're talking about 1995, which we'd made
a lot of games since 1990, 20 something games.

01:13:46.440 --> 01:13:53.630
So with Quake, we're creating a new engine,
one that can do full 3D, one that has a completely

01:13:53.630 --> 01:13:59.370
different look to it, has more advanced gameplay
code.

01:13:59.370 --> 01:14:00.760
Everything about it was different.

01:14:00.760 --> 01:14:07.830
When we got to November of that year, the
engine was finally ready, but the team was

01:14:07.830 --> 01:14:16.010
burnt out because of the massive effort that
it took to create the technology and the fact

01:14:16.010 --> 01:14:19.770
that they'd been making a lot of data that
couldn't be used because the engine changed

01:14:19.770 --> 01:14:24.430
so much that what they made was too primitive.

01:14:24.430 --> 01:14:27.240
The engine could do more than what they had
created.

01:14:27.240 --> 01:14:31.760
Because every time we were making stuff for
the game, the engine would be at a certain

01:14:31.760 --> 01:14:36.690
point of speed and we're modifying our levels
to make it run well at that speed.

01:14:36.690 --> 01:14:40.550
But that means it's a little too primitive
because the engine got faster and faster and

01:14:40.550 --> 01:14:41.550
faster.

01:14:41.550 --> 01:14:48.580
And by the time we reached November, we could
render much more interesting views in the

01:14:48.580 --> 01:14:50.380
world, but we didn't build those worlds yet.

01:14:50.380 --> 01:14:53.120
We were slowly inching towards them.

01:14:53.120 --> 01:14:58.330
So it was a lot of like, wow, all the stuff
I worked on can't really use it.

01:14:58.330 --> 01:15:04.060
So people were burned out from that, and the
team did not feel like they were up to the

01:15:04.060 --> 01:15:12.489
task of doing research on a new style of game,
which is what Quake was going to be.

01:15:12.489 --> 01:15:14.330
It was not going to be a first-person shooter.

01:15:14.330 --> 01:15:18.409
They basically suggested that we should just
make a shooter because we know how to do it.

01:15:18.409 --> 01:15:19.969
We can nail it.

01:15:19.969 --> 01:15:22.639
This is brand new tech that's awesome already.

01:15:22.639 --> 01:15:27.730
If we just do a shooter, we can all just ship
a game and take a rest kind of thing.

01:15:27.730 --> 01:15:30.050
And I was just like, "No, we need to do this.

01:15:30.050 --> 01:15:31.810
The whole world knows what this game is.

01:15:31.810 --> 01:15:33.370
It's in magazines.

01:15:33.370 --> 01:15:38.830
Everybody knows what Quake is, and this is
what I'm all about is we're pushing our technology

01:15:38.830 --> 01:15:39.890
to the limit.

01:15:39.890 --> 01:15:41.590
Let's push design to the limit too.

01:15:41.590 --> 01:15:46.300
Let's go beyond what we've done before and
make something new people haven't seen."

01:15:46.300 --> 01:15:51.570
I wanted to make this 3D game experience something
no one had seen before that goes beyond our

01:15:51.570 --> 01:15:53.179
first-person shooter.

01:15:53.179 --> 01:16:01.840
You know, our forte, our expertise was in
shooters, so how can we go beyond the shooter

01:16:01.840 --> 01:16:05.210
and do something that incorporates elements
of that that goes beyond it?

01:16:05.210 --> 01:16:13.400
And so they just really didn't want to do
another unknowable mountain to climb.

01:16:13.400 --> 01:16:15.640
When is it going to be defined?

01:16:15.640 --> 01:16:19.420
We don't have a scope that has an end on it
now.

01:16:19.420 --> 01:16:26.020
We didn't either with the engine and we didn't
change course while we're doing it either,

01:16:26.020 --> 01:16:28.739
which is what really harmed the team.

01:16:28.739 --> 01:16:34.010
So I basically just gave in and said, "I will
design a first-person shooter that will use

01:16:34.010 --> 01:16:40.790
all of this stuff and what we've done to date
to the best that we can, and then release

01:16:40.790 --> 01:16:42.679
it as fast as we possibly can."

01:16:42.679 --> 01:16:46.320
So we started doing that from the middle of
November.

01:16:46.320 --> 01:16:51.330
We basically went into crunch mode at that
time, even though everyone's burned out.

01:16:51.330 --> 01:16:56.560
They were mostly burned out because they weren't
making something that they could see what

01:16:56.560 --> 01:16:58.850
it was going to be used for.

01:16:58.850 --> 01:17:03.500
And because we wanted to get it done as fast
as possible, we went into what's called crunch

01:17:03.500 --> 01:17:09.100
mode, which at that time was seven days a
week, all the hours that you can, which is

01:17:09.100 --> 01:17:12.370
at least 10 a day.

01:17:12.370 --> 01:17:15.120
And we did that for seven months, seven days
a week.

01:17:15.120 --> 01:17:16.570
Tim Ferriss: Oh, my God.

01:17:16.570 --> 01:17:19.290
John Romero: And it was really hard.

01:17:19.290 --> 01:17:24.390
And after the game was released, it was like
a month and a half before I quit because there

01:17:24.390 --> 01:17:29.660
was still some work for me to get the game
mastered and created to go into stores and

01:17:29.660 --> 01:17:31.889
be shipped to customers, that kind of thing.

01:17:31.889 --> 01:17:36.060
I took a vacation, I came back, I mastered
all that stuff, and I was already planning

01:17:36.060 --> 01:17:42.330
for half a year to leave because if we're
not going to commit ourselves to furthering

01:17:42.330 --> 01:17:47.270
design, it's just going to become a technology
company, then I want to leave and go create

01:17:47.270 --> 01:17:48.909
a design company.

01:17:48.909 --> 01:17:54.540
And so I've already planned after that big
meeting about a month later, two months later,

01:17:54.540 --> 01:17:58.330
already planned to leave and start another
company with Tom Hall.

01:17:58.330 --> 01:18:00.890
And he agreed that he would love to do that.

01:18:00.890 --> 01:18:06.060
So I said, after shipping Quake, then we'll
start doing that.

01:18:06.060 --> 01:18:07.230
So that's what happened.

01:18:07.230 --> 01:18:16.500
After shipping, I started getting in touch
with publishers and they also—John Carmack

01:18:16.500 --> 01:18:21.410
mainly—didn't want to work with me anymore,
so they asked for my resignation.

01:18:21.410 --> 01:18:26.050
I was like, "Well, I'm going anyway, so let's
sign it."

01:18:26.050 --> 01:18:28.780
But it was a very tough game to get through.

01:18:28.780 --> 01:18:34.960
Tim Ferriss: What was that experience of separating
from the company like for you?

01:18:34.960 --> 01:18:37.610
Was it challenging?

01:18:37.610 --> 01:18:45.520
Was it strange to be on your own and to leave
behind maybe the identity that you had built

01:18:45.520 --> 01:18:46.760
with that team?

01:18:46.760 --> 01:18:51.090
What was that experience just emotionally,
psychologically for you?

01:18:51.090 --> 01:18:58.540
John Romero: Leaving and starting another
company, I was very excited about it.

01:18:58.540 --> 01:19:04.460
I was excited because I was going to be doing
this with Tom, who's a really great friend,

01:19:04.460 --> 01:19:09.719
amazing creative force, someone super fun
to work with.

01:19:09.719 --> 01:19:12.060
And we both had tons and tons of experience.

01:19:12.060 --> 01:19:14.000
So I was really excited about that.

01:19:14.000 --> 01:19:17.870
But I really liked working with the other
people at it as well.

01:19:17.870 --> 01:19:22.810
So it was really, it sucked, but it was also
exciting, like at the same time.

01:19:22.810 --> 01:19:28.980
It was like that was a lot of really great
work there but I'm also excited about what

01:19:28.980 --> 01:19:36.570
I can possibly do with without limitations
on what, say, design could do.

01:19:36.570 --> 01:19:40.830
So it was negatives and positives, but I think
that it was mostly positive because we're

01:19:40.830 --> 01:19:43.300
starting to do something new.

01:19:43.300 --> 01:19:45.810
And that was really, really fun to do with
Tom.

01:19:45.810 --> 01:19:53.390
And we spent about half a year building relationships
with different publishers to start Ion Storm.

01:19:53.390 --> 01:19:59.440
And finally after six months, figure out who
we're going to side with and signed up with

01:19:59.440 --> 01:20:00.440
Eidos.

01:20:00.440 --> 01:20:08.100
Tim Ferriss: What led you to want to write
your memoir, DOOM Guy: Life in First Person?

01:20:08.100 --> 01:20:13.290
What were the reasons for wanting to write
this book and publish this book?

01:20:13.290 --> 01:20:22.179
John Romero: So several years ago, I went
to do a speaking engagement in Canada, and

01:20:22.179 --> 01:20:30.810
they basically had heard the story of DOOM's
development and already knew some of the stuff

01:20:30.810 --> 01:20:31.810
I talked about.

01:20:31.810 --> 01:20:33.730
So they said, "Can you do something different?

01:20:33.730 --> 01:20:39.810
Can you tell us about your early life and
what made you the person that would make DOOM.

01:20:39.810 --> 01:20:44.040
Instead talking about DOOM talk about how
you became the person to make DOOM."

01:20:44.040 --> 01:20:46.910
So it was like, oh, kind of my life in games,
early games.

01:20:46.910 --> 01:20:50.040
So it's like, all right, I'll write that talk.

01:20:50.040 --> 01:20:55.590
So I basically wrote a talk that had a lot
of the stuff that happened in my early childhood

01:20:55.590 --> 01:21:04.350
with all of the exciting things that had to
do with drugs and murders and just the low

01:21:04.350 --> 01:21:07.350
income stuff, which kind of shocked people.

01:21:07.350 --> 01:21:10.230
They had never heard any of that before.

01:21:10.230 --> 01:21:16.160
And hearing the audience and the questions
later was just like people saying, "You should

01:21:16.160 --> 01:21:17.660
put in a book.

01:21:17.660 --> 01:21:18.880
Nobody knows about that information."

01:21:18.880 --> 01:21:23.219
I was like, "Yeah, I didn't really ever talk
about it before."

01:21:23.219 --> 01:21:30.540
And that would be a good idea in fact, because
I haven't seen anything out there that really

01:21:30.540 --> 01:21:38.600
documents software's ludography, really like
what game started on what date, when did that

01:21:38.600 --> 01:21:45.060
game ship, what's the authoritative book on
the development of all of this software?

01:21:45.060 --> 01:21:48.010
Stuff I could actually put that in this history
as well.

01:21:48.010 --> 01:21:54.990
So it was kind of a twofold reason for writing
the book was to get the whole story out there

01:21:54.990 --> 01:22:03.400
from when I was born until today, and to also
be a really great chronicle for everything

01:22:03.400 --> 01:22:08.440
that happened during those years, and when
those things happened, and a bunch of the

01:22:08.440 --> 01:22:12.980
cool stories that people hadn't heard before,
and just put a lot of really good stuff in

01:22:12.980 --> 01:22:14.570
there as well.

01:22:14.570 --> 01:22:20.460
There was a game that I was working on called
Blackroom, and every once in a while I get

01:22:20.460 --> 01:22:22.830
people asking, "So what's up with Blackroom?"

01:22:22.830 --> 01:22:25.560
And I always tell them, "I can't talk about
Blackroom."

01:22:25.560 --> 01:22:26.850
Well, Blackroom's in this book.

01:22:26.850 --> 01:22:30.160
So people are going to get to hear everything
about Blackroom.

01:22:30.160 --> 01:22:36.390
Tim Ferriss: Is it true that DOOM, the name,
comes from The Color of Money, or is the origin

01:22:36.390 --> 01:22:39.110
a different origin story for the name?

01:22:39.110 --> 01:22:44.190
John Romero: Yeah, the name for DOOM came
from John Carmack.

01:22:44.190 --> 01:22:51.680
He was watching Tom Cruise's The Color of
Money movie and when he was watching it and

01:22:51.680 --> 01:23:00.730
he saw that the guy walk up to Tom Cruise,
he's got his cue stick inside of this case

01:23:00.730 --> 01:23:01.940
on his lap.

01:23:01.940 --> 01:23:03.940
And the guy's like, "What's in there?"

01:23:03.940 --> 01:23:07.400
And Tom Cruise looks down at it and looks
up at the guy and goes, "It's doom."

01:23:07.400 --> 01:23:10.900
He was like, "Wow, that's a really great name."

01:23:10.900 --> 01:23:13.980
We hadn't heard of a game called DOOM before.

01:23:13.980 --> 01:23:15.550
So he said, "How about DOOM?"

01:23:15.550 --> 01:23:17.960
And I was like, "That is awesome.

01:23:17.960 --> 01:23:18.960
Yeah."

01:23:18.960 --> 01:23:19.960
Tim Ferriss: And that was it.

01:23:19.960 --> 01:23:22.130
The rest is history.

01:23:22.130 --> 01:23:27.830
So John, people can find you on Twitter @romero,
on Instagram @theromero.

01:23:27.830 --> 01:23:30.540
Thank you for taking the time.

01:23:30.540 --> 01:23:34.360
And is there anything else that you'd like
to mention?

01:23:34.360 --> 01:23:39.960
Any other asks or requests of the audience,
anything at all that you'd like to mention

01:23:39.960 --> 01:23:42.620
before we bring this to a close?

01:23:42.620 --> 01:23:48.980
John Romero: Well, I mean, I'm really grateful
to have had a really fun career making games.

01:23:48.980 --> 01:23:57.540
It's been a blast from the early days of learning
how to program a computer all the way to today

01:23:57.540 --> 01:24:03.239
using really advanced technology to make shooters
with bigger teams.

01:24:03.239 --> 01:24:11.320
I've seen everything that's happened during
that huge span of time, and it's been just

01:24:11.320 --> 01:24:12.530
a really, really, really great time overall.

01:24:12.530 --> 01:24:19.000
So I'm just really just happy to have had
that kind of a life.

01:24:19.000 --> 01:24:21.230
And all of that is basically in this book.

01:24:21.230 --> 01:24:22.230
Tim Ferriss: Excellent.

01:24:22.230 --> 01:24:25.960
So John, thank you so much for taking the
time and I am looking forward to getting a

01:24:25.960 --> 01:24:27.659
copy of DOOM Guy.

01:24:27.659 --> 01:24:30.350
People will be able to find it where books
are sold.

01:24:30.350 --> 01:24:36.790
And we'll link to everything in the show notes
as per usual to all my fine listeners at tim.blog/podcast.

01:24:36.790 --> 01:24:42.520
And until next time, be just a little bit
kinder to yourself and to others.

01:24:42.520 --> 01:24:44.660
And as always, thanks for tuning in.

